[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-161830"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/assets/fd18335a2034613658c65186c4fc6e3e74067e730183d3e66fbacf8ab3601928.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,z,\\,-y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,R=-y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-2x)-(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-2y)+(-y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,z,-y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-161862"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/assets/e61f3d7c23ef7876bec2f11c93974551058bcd32f28745898604926d84dbcfee.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨y,\\,0,\\,2x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨y,0,2x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cone ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2)=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=2x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-0\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-161899/selection/e7190fb696a776020675bdb92c182bdb27f1c78a0e6fbb2226874175aa87da6a" :answer/id #uuid "4a4ad1e8-fb8d-5045-adb0-4a637a9753ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{y-x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161899/selection/be29ae730677ee9c4902153465298467b16f11fd1cf18fa807a63f86fc61f3c8" :answer/id #uuid "7203265a-4390-527f-b95e-1643e43c34b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x+y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161899/selection/66d7fb2d8c08e2f0127eb73fde4ee86124a709d5a6e8874afed43591ba8c2f86" :answer/id #uuid "4a069c36-b5b0-52d6-809f-19d65bb38ead" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{xy-y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161899/selection/770ce58be2ce08e9e4b6e9c503accc13eb41dcc046c780829e2c6c82ca2c7b14" :answer/id #uuid "76433b57-2b2c-51bd-8789-cbff918f080c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161899/selection/a7c5bfbd0d66ec53c37901150cb01e412d4cf00c96ce07e9171bec60b04073c1" :answer/id #uuid "d7690d3c-75eb-5fbd-92ed-d1158ea79456" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161899/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "279c2708-6652-5ba9-a0ae-e0281f94d404" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161899/selection/770ce58be2ce08e9e4b6e9c503accc13eb41dcc046c780829e2c6c82ca2c7b14" :answer-field/choices ["q-161899/selection/e7190fb696a776020675bdb92c182bdb27f1c78a0e6fbb2226874175aa87da6a" "q-161899/selection/be29ae730677ee9c4902153465298467b16f11fd1cf18fa807a63f86fc61f3c8" "q-161899/selection/66d7fb2d8c08e2f0127eb73fde4ee86124a709d5a6e8874afed43591ba8c2f86" "q-161899/selection/770ce58be2ce08e9e4b6e9c503accc13eb41dcc046c780829e2c6c82ca2c7b14" "q-161899/selection/a7c5bfbd0d66ec53c37901150cb01e412d4cf00c96ce07e9171bec60b04073c1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161899" :question/id #uuid "62cc77f5-d29c-5b66-aeee-e4c2bff38586" :question/math-academy-id "q-161899" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/assets/8a48be0b4c101babf157a05959e923576ad5614113b3eec9e93fe747d5dc3803.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,x,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=x,\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨1,x,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A+0 \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161899/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161899"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161903/selection/f10bd8ca4d389454da07ba5e17801dd8ef8e6cd1578ef5319a9a0feb3f536bfa" :answer/id #uuid "5ce8bf71-8f80-5f4d-97e5-01363250f04d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y-\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161903/selection/0d04ae6d410bda02675303eeaf21d24093884ae59769c4fd91d89de2050e1007" :answer/id #uuid "ed3b0302-28ab-5be2-86c7-e2840bfa6cf0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161903/selection/1de97639243118fe2a274f2b36c7fce214d295b8fe0b9d6a4051f01ddde87644" :answer/id #uuid "b4eec892-0639-5e9d-ae67-55af7c915d07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2y+\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161903/selection/db33d5e33d866816d978388239c446f055c045ab4ba2f3dfe758f5e924eb78a9" :answer/id #uuid "8798600b-a935-5e72-af8a-7ef618e4933c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4y+\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161903/selection/79d05933a3fa591e9bae61d916771b806b6b69bfe48ac4fa336831eca3fb8af7" :answer/id #uuid "1514334d-b962-51ff-bc04-fd2348c7a74a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161903/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8924d504-3edb-5802-9332-5109510777e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161903/selection/79d05933a3fa591e9bae61d916771b806b6b69bfe48ac4fa336831eca3fb8af7" :answer-field/choices ["q-161903/selection/f10bd8ca4d389454da07ba5e17801dd8ef8e6cd1578ef5319a9a0feb3f536bfa" "q-161903/selection/0d04ae6d410bda02675303eeaf21d24093884ae59769c4fd91d89de2050e1007" "q-161903/selection/1de97639243118fe2a274f2b36c7fce214d295b8fe0b9d6a4051f01ddde87644" "q-161903/selection/db33d5e33d866816d978388239c446f055c045ab4ba2f3dfe758f5e924eb78a9" "q-161903/selection/79d05933a3fa591e9bae61d916771b806b6b69bfe48ac4fa336831eca3fb8af7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161903" :question/id #uuid "c8fd3ad8-a86b-542f-ab4a-6226468a4f6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161903" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,-z,\\,2y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the cone $z=4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=0.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=-z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4,\\,R=2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(0)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,-z,2y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161903/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161903"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161915/selection/77f57ef14c8c9bbbf93d226fe49f7240a7ac78ed2180222a97fb9d512f656b12" :answer/id #uuid "0ba42783-544e-5b07-94f7-1a6b12ab1787" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4xy+2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161915/selection/7f76b41daa2aaf84d7cfbe0593c03ea0ed68031fa8af849f70e4fea2707b122e" :answer/id #uuid "2fdc2d1f-f3e4-5c82-81f2-99ded537b932" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "xy+2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161915/selection/2655e9ce71f7d00cc6d28957d9b86c56fb4861819d4259712acb1b4d1028661a" :answer/id #uuid "d2d8e0a8-8a62-5921-a1dc-3804fb6ca864" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4xy-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161915/selection/00704ad257ec9b5e2bb5be1f48141e4283959429ce3adf41ba52f8c367d0b41e" :answer/id #uuid "d9a1b88a-014d-541f-ba9a-48c84dbaf1ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161915/selection/8530e8b25ab4e6163631250ca4a03c520bc937536b0c79d684f02642b028dff1" :answer/id #uuid "14d84f94-039f-5b5f-9505-53de3f5d6e12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161915/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "df3fc501-8aec-587f-8354-4407ad8c7b26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161915/selection/77f57ef14c8c9bbbf93d226fe49f7240a7ac78ed2180222a97fb9d512f656b12" :answer-field/choices ["q-161915/selection/77f57ef14c8c9bbbf93d226fe49f7240a7ac78ed2180222a97fb9d512f656b12" "q-161915/selection/7f76b41daa2aaf84d7cfbe0593c03ea0ed68031fa8af849f70e4fea2707b122e" "q-161915/selection/2655e9ce71f7d00cc6d28957d9b86c56fb4861819d4259712acb1b4d1028661a" "q-161915/selection/00704ad257ec9b5e2bb5be1f48141e4283959429ce3adf41ba52f8c367d0b41e" "q-161915/selection/8530e8b25ab4e6163631250ca4a03c520bc937536b0c79d684f02642b028dff1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161915" :question/id #uuid "1c5fb359-1792-5bae-84b5-842bbac13d34" :question/math-academy-id "q-161915" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨y,\\,0,\\,-z⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloids $z=1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (-2x)-(0)\\cdot (-2y)+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (2x)-(0)\\cdot (2y)+4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-2xy+4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy+2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-5\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-161915/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161915"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
